---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Teaching - Stephen Zweibel"
  description="Courses and programs on programming, data analysis, and agentic AI for researchers and librarians"
>
  <header class="page-header">
    <h1>Teaching</h1>
    <p>
      I teach graduate students and librarians how to use computational tools while keeping enough understanding to question, inspect, and repair what those tools produce.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2>Graduate courses</h2>
    <div class="courses">
      <article class="course">
        <h3>AI-Assisted Programming</h3>
        <p class="course-meta">DHUM 78000 / DATA 78000 · Spring and Fall 2026 · 1 credit</p>
        <p>
          Students use coding agents to build small web projects, then investigate the code through file tracing, browser inspection, change-and-observe exercises, documentation checks, and debugging. The course is designed for students without prior programming experience.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="course">
        <h3>Python for Researchers</h3>
        <p class="course-meta">Fall semesters · 1 credit</p>
        <p>
          A project-oriented introduction to Python for academic research, including data analysis with pandas, APIs, Jupyter notebooks, visualization, and AI-assisted coding practices.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="course">
        <h3>Introduction to JavaScript</h3>
        <p class="course-meta">DATA 70600 / DHUM 70600 · Spring semesters · 1 credit</p>
        <p>
          An introduction to JavaScript and web development for humanities and social science researchers, taught through small projects and data visualizations.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Programs and workshops</h2>
    <article class="program">
      <h3>Agentic AI for Library Practice</h3>
      <p class="course-meta">CUNY Office of Library Services · Spring 2026</p>
      <p>
        I led this 16-week peer mentoring program for CUNY librarians. Participants built tools for real library problems and learned to verify agent claims against source code, official documentation, and the systems they already knew. The <a href="/presentations/showcase-spring-2026.html">showcase presentation</a> records the first cohort’s work.
      </p>
    </article>
    <p>
      I also lead workshops on command-line tools, Python, JavaScript, APIs, databases, text analysis, data management, HTML, and CSS. At the Digital Humanities Summer Institute I taught Web APIs with Python in 2018, 2019, and 2024, and Introduction to JavaScript in 2025.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Open teaching materials</h2>
    <ul class="resource-list">
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/szweibel/DHSI-API-workshop" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Web APIs with Python</a>
        <span> — DHSI workshop materials for finding, requesting, and working with API data.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="http://zweibel.net/Javascripting-Graduate-Student/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Javascripting Graduate Student</a>
        <span> — An interactive JavaScript curriculum for humanities researchers.</span>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://cuny-ai-lab.github.io/ach-2026/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Building Community-Oriented Infrastructures for AI Experimentation</a>
        <span> — The CUNY AI Lab’s ACH 2026 panel deck, including the teaching and infrastructure behind the lab.</span>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-header { margin-bottom: 3rem; }
  .page-header p { color: var(--color-text-muted); }
  section { margin-top: 3rem; }
  .courses { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1rem; }
  .course, .program { padding: 1.25rem; border: 1px solid var(--color-border); background: var(--color-bg-alt); }
  .course h3, .program h3 { font-size: 1.15rem; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
  .course-meta { color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .course p:last-child, .program p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .resource-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; }
  .resource-list li { border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border); padding: 0.75rem 0; }
  .resource-list span { color: var(--color-text-muted); }
</style>
